--- note
## The puzzle
The competition ground truth is **sparse**: the official data page says *"annotations are sparse — not every
cell in every frame is labeled."* Measured across all 199 training movies, only **~1–3%** of detectable nuclei
carry a GT track. For a long time we could describe *what* those cells look like (isolated, well-separated,
long continuous lineages) but not *why* they — and only they — were chosen.

This lesson closes that gap with the **actual protocol from the source papers**, read through the
`research-search` agent's full-text mode, then **proven against our own data** by the `paper-verify` agent.

--- note
### See it first — the real frames
Left: a **sparse** 6bba movie (~3.8k cells/frame, early stage). Right: a **dense** 44b6 movie (~79k cells/frame,
late stage). Green = **every** nucleus a detector finds. Red = the **GT-labeled** cells. In the dense crop,
**1 of ~1000** visible nuclei is labeled. The sparsity is not subtle.
--- image
learning/label_figs/label_frame_overlay.png
Real max-Z projections. Green = detected nuclei; red = the sparse GT lineage cells. The ~1% is a property of how the GT was MADE, not of which cells are "special".

--- note
### The source
The data is carved from **Zebrahub** (Lange et al., *Cell* 2024) and its tracking GT is generated by the
**Ultrack** dual-channel sparse-labeling protocol (Royer et al., *Nature Methods* 2025, PMC12615266 — open
access). The Ultrack methods, quoted verbatim, describe exactly how the ~1% is produced.
--- shape
Zebrahub — <b>Cell 2024</b><br>doi 10.1016/j.cell.2024.09.047<br><br>Ultrack — <b>Nat. Methods 2025</b><br>PMC12615266 (OA)

--- note
### The protocol — two fluorescent channels in ONE embryo
1. **Ubiquitous green** `Tg(ef1α:H2B-mNeonGreen)` labels **every** nucleus — this is the image we detect on.
2. **Sparse red** `pMTB-ef1-H2B-mCherry`, delivered by **Tol2 microinjection at ~4 hpf**, integrates into a
   **random ~20–30%** of cells and is *"clonally inherited... propagates to daughter cells upon division."*
3. The sparse-red cells are *"automatically tracked using Ultrack and then manually curated"* into
   *"high-quality, 'platinum-level' annotations"* — *"selecting long, green-overlapping, high-quality lineages."*
4. Those curated red lineages become the GT, used to *"evaluate... the ubiquitously labeled [green] channel"* —
   **our exact task.**
--- image
learning/label_figs/label_protocol_schematic.png
The five steps from mosaic injection to the sparse "platinum" ground truth we are scored against.

--- note
### Why this rewrites the "isolated cells" story
Our earlier data-only guess was *"annotators pick isolated, trackable cells."* The paper shows the causal truth:
**isolation is a side-effect of mosaic sparsity**, not a selection rule. Only 20–30% of nuclei carry the marker
(far fewer per small crop), so labeled cells are *physically* sparse and therefore look isolated. The clonal
inheritance also explains why the GT is **complete lineage trees of a few founder cells** — the founders are the
cells whose ancestor received the integration.
--- shape
random mosaic 20–30%<br>→ clonally inherited<br>→ curated long lineages<br>→ <b>~1% platinum GT</b>

